    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49972.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 53,
    "turn_text": "We we had a wonderful dinner last night sandy saffley hosted us where we we did what we call the jefferson dinner and anybody's interested at all in doing this at your home. Grab me later because it was it was very informative very enlightening. The goal was to not argue the goal was to have one common conversation and find common ground and we did it was very diverse lot of diverse opinions we all agreed on one thing about what you're getting to which is. I think we all understand what the problem is. And let me state it in some statistics. I've been working with Glenn's firm, Stern's waiver on this, and I want to share this with you. In 1980, there's two groups I want to share. One is the National Journal, which scores the members of Congress, the ones that scored you in the center, and the other one is Pew Research. National Journal at the time, in 1980, said 80% of the members of Congress were scored as bipartisan. Ronald Reagan's first term, 80% of the members crossed party lines to vote with the other party. At the time, Pew, if you go back and look at Pew Research, they scored, because we always say, well, it's the American people who are more divided than ever. 80% of Americans. Congress, 80% of Americans are bipartisan. Today, few research, same scale, same set of questions. 80% of Americans are bipartisan. We still believe hard work, fair play, we don't like taxes, but we pay them. We believe in giving somebody a hand up when they need it, et cetera, right? We still hold those same values. You can look at things like abortion, death penalty, marijuana is the exception there, but 80% of Americans then, 80% of Congress then, Two and a half percent, only 11 members of Congress today are considered bipartisan. That's the problem, right? How do we get here? I got the easy part. How do we get here, Jason?",
