    "turn_idx": 146,
    "turn_text": "to answer Robert's question a different way, because you get to something important. I think the issue for me of all voters vote on this top two primary, for me, it's not about voter apathy. That's an important issue, but so is water quality. To me, this issue is something different. This won't solve that issue. But I will say this. If you went into an ice cream store, and the only flavors were vanilla, or French vanilla, or French vanilla bean, and you went to another store, it was three variations of strawberry, you're less likely to go to an ice cream store. Now, if you go to lofty pursuits, anybody here in big lofty pursuits, they make all this kind of incredible variety of ice creams. It makes you want to go. But what we have now in the Democratic primary, and I've done over 200 Democratic primary races, trust me, there's that much difference between them. And I've worked on dozens and dozens and dozens of Republican primaries, and there's that much difference between them. I don't blame voters for missing primaries. And where are the turnout the highest? In gubernatorial general elections, state of Florida, go to the Supervisor of Elections website. It's between 48 and 50 percent. Every cycle. Presidential, it's about 75%. What do you got there? You have options in a presidential election. You have options in a gubernatorial election. You really don't have options in a primary A and B, nonpartisan voters are told over and over again, there's nothing for you on the ballot. Yet, ironically, all you lawyers, we put judge races there. What does that tell you? So, judges are only being chosen by... Yes, non-partisans are legally allowed to go vote and you get this little ballot. Everybody else gets a nice big ballot. You get, you know, Judge Sheffield Retainer or not. List, we have more questions.",
