  {
    "episode_id": 49972,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49972.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 152,
    "turn_text": "Hi, I'm Evelyn Shelley. I'm retired from the state of Florida. I get a little concerned when I keep hearing that certain legislators are sort of committed to different industry viewpoints, different business viewpoints, because of the way their campaigns have been financed. If you have a lot more candidates in the open primary kind of situation, would that just ensure that those who are particularly well financed by industry and business are going to be elected.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "I am Evelyn Shelley.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "I am retired from the state of Florida.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "I feel concern when I hear that some legislators are influenced by industry and business viewpoints due to campaign financing.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Campaign financing influences legislative alignment with industry and business interests.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "An increase in candidates during open primaries may lead to greater election of candidates backed by industry and business funding.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Legislators' policy decisions are shaped by financial support from industries and businesses.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Well-financed candidates have a disproportionate advantage in elections.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "The public has a right to expect impartiality from its representatives despite campaign funding sources.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Open primary systems tend to favor candidates with stronger financial backing.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Industry and business interests represent distinct and potentially conflicting viewpoints from the general public.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Topic Shift"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 49972,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49972.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 153,
    "turn_text": "Well, let me give you the answer is no matter what type of election process you have, that's always going to be the case. The people who are the incumbents or who are cozying up to the industries are going to get money from those industries and they're going to dominate. The incumbent advantage is based upon that principle, right? You can attract outside funding. But let me talk about campaign finance for a moment. This is an interesting study that was done that will depress you, but not surprise you. They looked at members of Congress, U.S. House and senators, and their voting record, and they polled their constituents, the people that they represent in their state or district, and their views on issues, and they looked at their sources of funding and their campaigns. And it turns out, maybe unsurprisingly, that their voting record aligned much more closely to the people who gave them money than the people that they're representing. That's disturbing. We spent over six and a half billion dollars in 2016 on federal elections, two and a half on the presidential race, four on the lower level race, Congress and Senate. of that money, 39% of all the money we spent in the entire country on federal elections, in the biggest, most expensive election we've had in our history, 39% of that money came from people in the 0.01% income bracket. not 1%, not 0.1%, 0.01, which is basically a couple hundred people. Spent 39% of the money that we use to influence federal elections. That is a huge problem and it distorts the process.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_05",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "In any election process, incumbents or those aligned with industries will receive money from those industries.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "The 2016 U.S. federal elections cost over six and a half billion dollars.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "Incumbent advantage stems from the ability to attract outside funding.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Of the 2016 federal election spending, $2.5 billion was spent on the presidential race.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Of the 2016 federal election spending, $4 billion was spent on congressional and senatorial races.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "A study found that members of Congress vote more in line with their donors than with their constituents.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "In 2016, 39% of federal election funds came from individuals in the top 0.01% income bracket.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "The top 0.01% income bracket consists of roughly a few hundred people.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "The concentration of political funding among the ultra-rich distorts the democratic electoral process.",
