  {
    "episode_id": 49972,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49972.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 154,
    "turn_text": "I agree with you that money permeates the system. You said early on when you vote, you have three things, your conscience, what you've studied, what you've learned, your constituents. And then to some degree, you're donor, people who supported you. But I think this system does is it up weights constituents. Because right now, if you're protected in a little bubble of partisanship, what happens there is the definition of partisanship both on the right and on the left is defined by a few key issues. The vast majority of issues that lawmakers are confronted with never make a lie today. never fall into a partisan bubble. Whether you're going to fund repairs to the C-51 Canal going into Lake Worth, or you're going to fund a straightening of the Kissimmee River, voters aren't going to say one way or the other, but there are financial interests. The cattle industry on one, maybe the sugar industry on the other, may have different viewpoints about that. So they're hiring their team of lobbyists and they're making donations to people irrespective of that point of partisanship. When you go back home and you're a Democrat, it's, you know, how did you vote on these four or five or six Democratic litmus issues by opening up the primaries in this regard. And I don't want this to be all about local primaries, but you then have to answer to a broader array of people and say, why didn't you do this? Why didn't you do that? So, I just think it changes the equation a little bit. But there's a whole slew of things. You want to talk more about some of the ideas on campaign finance, people? I mean, you should go there a",
