    "turn_text": "I'm not related to money. I have an interesting recommendation in the book of when we elect the Speaker of the House, I think you should need 60 percent of the vote of the House rather than 50 percent. And you might say, okay, so what does that matter? That means you're going to have to get people from the other party to support Your nominee for Speaker to have any chance to win. Look at a couple of our most polarizing recent speakers. When Newt Gingrich became Speaker in 1995, if that were the process, he would have needed 31 Democrats to support it. Never would have happened. When Nancy Pelosi became Speaker in 2007, she would have needed 28 Republicans.",
